# Packages to install, read by bin/system/install-packages.
#
# One entry per line. A bare name is the same package on apt and brew.
# Windows ignores bare names; its packages need scoop or winget entries.
# Prefixes limit an entry to one manager:
#   apt:NAME   brew:NAME   cask:NAME (macOS GUI apps)
#   scoop:NAME (Windows CLI tools; BUCKET/NAME for a bucket other than main)
#   winget:ID (Windows apps, including Microsoft Store ones)
#   npm:NAME   uv:NAME
#   custom:NAME   apt-custom:NAME   brew-custom:NAME
# A custom entry runs the script bin/packages/install-NAME, as does a package
# unavailable from its manager, if such a script exists. Each script must
# support --check, exiting 0 when its tool is installed and current.
# An npm entry followed by the word scripts may run its install scripts,
# which npm otherwise warns about, and will someday refuse to run.
#
# [sections]: core always installs. gui installs on desktops only.
# The rest are opt-in, via --with SECTION or the machine's profile file
# (~/.config/dotfiles/sections, written by setup.sh):
#   build   compilers and build tools, for building software from source
#   dev     development tools beyond the basics
#   heavy   large installs (hundreds of MB), for primary machines
#   java    JDKs and Java tooling
#   system  hardware and network utilities
# A section header may name sections it requires, e.g. [dev: build].

